---
import CaseExplorer from "../../components/CaseExplorer.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { profile, publicSafetyNote } from "../../data/profile";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const pageTitle = `Purchase With Purchase Promo | ${profile.name}`;
const pageDescription =
  "Case study about launching ruparupa's Purchase With Purchase promotion before the 12.12 campaign.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <a class="back-link" href={base}>Back to portfolio</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">ruparupa · Product Manager · Product expansion</p>
      <h1>Purchase With Purchase Promo</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Designing a promotion system that supported multiple promotional SKUs under one Promo ID and launched in
        time for the 12.12 double-date campaign.
      </p>
      <div class="case-facts">
        <span>Project type: Product expansion</span>
        <span>Timeline: less than 2.5 months</span>
        <span>Outcome: IDR 81 million in revenue within the first 12 hours of 12.12</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <CaseExplorer initialSlug="purchase-with-purchase-promo" />

    <section class="content-section" id="full-story">
      <h2>Context</h2>
      <p>
        At ruparupa, the Marketing and Merchandising teams requested a Purchase With Purchase (PWP) promotion,
        where customers would become eligible to buy selected products at a discounted price after reaching a
        minimum transaction amount. The project was handed over to me after the previous Product Manager resigned.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Problem</h2>
      <h3>Requirements Problem</h3>
      <p>
        At the time, ruparupa's promotion system was designed to apply one promotional SKU to each Promo ID, such
        as for a direct discount, buy-one-get-one-free offer, or free gift. PWP introduced a new requirement: for
        the first time, a single Promo ID needed to support multiple promotional SKUs because customers could choose
        which discounted item to redeem after meeting the minimum transaction requirement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without changing this mechanism, the Marketing and Merchandising teams would have needed to create multiple
        Promo IDs for a single promotional program, which would undermine the purpose of maintaining one Promo ID
        per program. My challenge was therefore to design a promotion system that could support multiple SKUs under
        one Promo ID.
      </p>
      <h3>Timeline Problem</h3>
      <p>
        When the previous PM resigned, the requirements were still incomplete. I needed to reconfirm the existing
        requirements with stakeholders and define several requirements that had not yet been addressed. At the same
        time, when I began the requirement-gathering process, the Marketing team informed us that the promotion was
        expected to support the upcoming 12.12 double-date campaign, leaving us less than 2.5 months to deliver it.
      </p>
      <p>
        My engineers and I estimated that implementing the full inherited scope together with the newly identified
        requirements would take longer than the available timeframe. My challenge was therefore to define a feasible
        MVP and launch PWP in time for 12.12.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>My Role</h2>
      <p>As Product Manager, I coordinated the initiative across several stakeholder groups:</p>
      <ul class="two-column-list">
        <li>Engineering team: Backend, Frontend, and IT Infrastructure</li>
        <li>Quality Assurance (QA) team</li>
        <li>Marketing Promo team</li>
        <li>Merchandising team</li>
      </ul>
      <p>My responsibilities included:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Reconfirming the requirements inherited from the previous PM.</li>
        <li>Defining the remaining requirements that had not yet been specified.</li>
        <li>Breaking the scope into an MVP that could be launched before the 12.12 campaign.</li>
        <li>Coordinating with UI/UX to develop the high-fidelity design used during requirement grooming.</li>
        <li>Translating the requirements into a written PRD.</li>
        <li>Supporting development, SIT, and UAT.</li>
        <li>Communicating delivery status across stakeholders.</li>
        <li>Monitoring post-deployment product adoption and iteration.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section process-section">
      <h2>Product Requirements Quick Summary</h2>
      <p>
        One of the incomplete requirements involved the configuration view in the Marketing dashboard, specifically
        how to configure multiple promotional SKUs under one Promo ID. Because I was also a seller on Tokopedia,
        one of Indonesia's leading e-commerce platforms, I benchmarked its product-variant configuration pattern
        and adapted that concept to the multi-SKU promotion setup.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without a scalable configuration approach, the alternative requirement would have been to create multiple
        Promo IDs for a single PWP program, with one Promo ID for each SKU.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram-frame visual-frame">
        <img
          src={`${base}images/pwp-benefit-configuration.png`}
          alt="PWP dashboard benefit tab showing multiple promotional SKUs under one Promo ID"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Configuration concept for managing multiple promotional SKUs under one Promo ID.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Marketing and Merchandising users can bulk-edit all SKUs, apply changes only to selected SKUs, or edit an
        individual SKU while leaving the others unchanged. The checkbox on the left determines which SKUs are
        affected by the bulk edit. Selecting the checkbox in the top row selects all rows, while selecting
        individual row checkboxes limits the bulk changes to those SKUs.
      </p>
      <ul class="requirements-list">
        <li><strong>Maximum Qty:</strong> the maximum quantity of that SKU a customer can select on the PWP redemption page.</li>
        <li><strong>Quota:</strong> the maximum stock allocation of that SKU available for PWP redemption.</li>
        <li><strong>Discount / Final Price:</strong> determines whether the promotion uses a percentage discount or a fixed final price.</li>
        <li><strong>Amount:</strong> the discount percentage when Discount % is selected, or the final selling price when Final Price is selected.</li>
        <li><strong>Maximum Discount:</strong> enabled only for Discount %, defining the maximum discount amount in Indonesian Rupiah.</li>
        <li><strong>Maximum Item:</strong> the maximum number of PWP items a customer can redeem in a single checkout.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure class="diagram-frame visual-frame">
        <img
          src={`${base}images/pwp-terms-conditions.png`}
          alt="PWP dashboard T&C tab showing eligibility rules for minimum purchase, store ID, and item ID"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Eligibility requirements for the promotion are configured in the T&C tab.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Once a customer meets the minimum transaction requirement, the eligible promotional SKUs appear on the
        checkout page and become available for redemption.
      </p>
      <div class="visual-grid">
        <figure class="diagram-frame visual-frame">
          <img
            src={`${base}images/pwp-checkout-entry.png`}
            alt="Checkout page showing the Purchase With Purchase promo area"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Eligible promotional SKUs appear on the checkout page.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="diagram-frame visual-frame">
          <img
            src={`${base}images/pwp-redemption-modal.png`}
            alt="Tebus Murah redemption modal showing selectable discounted products"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Customers can select discounted PWP items from the redemption modal.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Project Management Challenge</h2>
      <p>
        When the project was handed over to me, the full scope was more complex than what was required for the
        immediate campaign. For example, the inherited scope included a Same Store configuration, which would
        require PWP items to be fulfilled from the same store as the main items used to meet the minimum purchase
        requirement. It also included five different final prices for each promotional SKU, corresponding to
        ruparupa's five geographic pricing zones, which reflected the distribution challenges of serving different
        areas across Indonesia.
      </p>
      <p>
        After reconfirming the requirements, we determined that these capabilities were not necessary for the
        upcoming 12.12 campaign. I therefore divided the development into phases: the team focused first on the
        requirements essential for the urgent double-date use case, while the remaining capabilities were deferred
        to subsequent roadmap phases.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section outcome-band">
      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <div class="outcome-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>1 week</strong>
          <p>before the 12.12 double-date campaign, we successfully launched the PWP promotion.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>IDR 81M</strong>
          <p>
            in revenue within the first 12 hours of the 12.12 campaign, making PWP one of ruparupa's most
            successful promotion launches on its first major day of use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>What I Learned</h2>
      <p>
        Taking over an inherited project taught me not to assume that existing requirements are complete or still
        reflect the current business need. Before continuing development, I needed to revalidate the inherited
        scope with stakeholders, understand what was still undefined, and rebuild enough context to make confident
        product decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also learned that meeting a hard commercial deadline requires deliberate scope management rather than
        simply pushing the full backlog faster. By separating the capabilities required for the 12.12 launch from
        more complex requirements such as Same Store and five-zone pricing, I could protect the core customer and
        business outcome while preserving the deferred requirements in the roadmap.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the project reinforced the value of looking outside the immediate product for proven interaction
        patterns. Benchmarking Tokopedia's product-variant configuration helped me frame a more scalable way for
        business users to manage multiple promotional SKUs under one Promo ID. For me, the lesson was not to copy
        another product, but to reuse a familiar interaction pattern when it solves a structurally similar problem.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section note-section">
      <p>{publicSafetyNote}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="contact-band">
      <h2>Interested in discussing product work?</h2>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="button primary" href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Email me</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={profile.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View LinkedIn</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
